DATA LICENSING (separate from the code licence above)

This repository's CODE is MIT-licensed. The DATA it fetches and derives is NOT,
and the three sources carry different obligations. Anything under data/, and any
GeoPackage, table or map this pipeline produces, is a derivative of all three.


1. OpenStreetMap  --  street network, schools, schoolyards, traffic calming

    (c) OpenStreetMap contributors
    https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright
    Open Database License (ODbL) v1.0
    https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/

ODbL is share-alike. Redistributing a derived database built from OSM requires
attributing OpenStreetMap and licensing that derived database under ODbL.


2. GHS-POP R2023A  --  residential population within school catchments

    European Commission, Joint Research Centre, Global Human Settlement Layer
    Schiavina M., Freire S., Carioli A., MacManus K. (2023):
    GHS-POP R2023A -- GHS population grid multitemporal (1975-2030)
    DOI 10.2905/2FF68A52-5B5B-4A22-8F40-C41DA8332CFE
    http://data.europa.eu/89h/2ff68a52-5b5b-4a22-8f40-c41da8332cfe

Released by the JRC as open and free data; reuse is authorised provided the
source is acknowledged.


3. Copernicus DEM GLO-30  --  elevation, for slope-adjusted walking speed

This pipeline reads a windowed extract and derives per-edge gradients from it,
so the data are *modified* and the licence's modified-data notice applies:

    produced using Copernicus WorldDEM-30 (c) DLR e.V. 2010-2014 and
    (c) Airbus Defence and Space GmbH 2014-2018 provided under COPERNICUS by the
    European Union and ESA; all rights reserved

Licence terms:
https://documentation.dataspace.copernicus.eu/APIs/SentinelHub/Data/DEM/resources/license/License-COPDEM-30.pdf

Note this is a Digital Surface Model, not a terrain model: it includes buildings
and vegetation. See notes/method_note.md section 4e for how that is handled.


If you redistribute any output of this pipeline -- including the maps under
docs/ -- all three notices above travel with it, and the ODbL share-alike
obligation applies to the derived database.
